-- title:
--   Projection onto transversal values and defect-class means
-- statement:
--   Let $\pi$ be a probability distribution on paired states $S\subseteq[n]\times\{0,1\}$, and let $H$ be a real observable. For an ordered defect class $D_{i,k}$, consisting of classifier states with empty pair $i$ and full pair $k$, define the projected observable by
--   $$\widehat H(S)=
--   \begin{cases}
--   H(S)&S\text{ is classified as transversal},\\
--   \displaystyle\frac{\sum_{T\in D_{i,k}}\pi(T)H(T)}
--   {\sum_{T\in D_{i,k}}\pi(T)}&S\text{ is classified as defect }(i,k),\\
--   0&S\text{ is classified as invalid}.
--   \end{cases}$$
--   Thus all states in a defect class receive its weighted conditional mean, while transversal values are retained individually. A zero-mass defect class has mean zero by total real division. The definition permits an arbitrary distribution $\pi$; it does not itself require invalid states to have zero mass.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/meelgroup/counting-matroid/blob/dd16a4628fdb095c58fe0f3dfd8e353c6e97443c/CountingMatroid/Analysis/ObservableProjection.lean#L13-L27

